# Footnotes

Two notes in one paragraph[^1] keep separate labels[^2], which is the
whole point of this file: with only one note a writer that loses the
count still looks right.

[^1]: The first body.
[^2]: The second body.

A body can hold more than a sentence[^long], and a label need not be a
number[^named].

[^long]: An opening paragraph.

    A second paragraph under the same note.

    - a bullet
    - another

[^named]: Labels are not part of the tree; only the order of first
    reference is.

The same note referenced twice[^twice] duplicates its body[^twice].

[^twice]: Referenced from two places.

A note inside *emphasis[^emph]*, inside a heading, inside a quote and
inside a list item — each keeps its place.

[^emph]: Inside emphasis.

## A heading with a note[^head]

[^head]: Attached to a heading.

> A quoted line with a note[^quote]

[^quote]: Attached to a quote.

- A list item with a note[^item]

[^item]: Attached to a list item.

| column |
|--------|
| a cell with a note[^cell] |

[^cell]: Attached to a table cell.

A definition may precede its use.

[^early]: Defined above where it is referenced.

Here is that reference[^early].
